<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cultivation System</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">from <a href="/years/1830">1830</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cultivation System was a policy introduced by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> from 1830, requiring villagers to devote part of their land or labour to export crops such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> for the state. It produced large profits for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>. It also caused great hardship and famine, and became a byword for exploitation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cultivation-system.jpg" alt="Cultivation System" width="320" height="267" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nicolaes Visscher II · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicolaes_Visscher_Indiae_Orientalis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
